WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
The final phase of the research lifecycle
is the communication of findings to

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.060
the scientific community. Researchers must
be transparent about the

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.600
limitations of their assumptions, ensuring
the robustness of the evidence is

4
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.680
not overstated. All research results,
including negative findings,

5
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.120
provide value to the record and must be
published or disseminated.

6
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.860
Authors must apply the same rigorous
criteria, whether they are publishing in

7
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.380
subscription journals or open access
platforms.

8
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.720
This uniformity of rigor extends to
preprint repositories as well.

9
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.300
Selectively reporting data or altering
standards based on the publication

10
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.080
venue directly conflicts with the
principle of honesty.

11
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.180
When successfully integrated, these
principles, environments,

12
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.400
and data protocols function as a unified
institutional blueprint.

13
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:49.160
The burden of maintaining this integrity
ecosystem is shared by authors,

14
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.860
journal editors, and publishers.

15
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.080
This stewardship includes the transparent
duty to correct the published record

16
00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.000
whenever necessary. The preservation of
empirical truth relies heavily on the

17
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.500
integration of human habits with these
external data frameworks.

18
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.320
Systemic adherence to the European Code of
Conduct for Research Integrity

19
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.160
provides the mechanism for guaranteeing
institutional trust.
